{
  "slug": "intersection-of-two-linked-lists",
  "title": "Intersection of Two Linked Lists",
  "topic": "linked-lists",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "Length alignment then lockstep two pointers",
  "statement": "Two singly linked lists are described by their node values: `a` lists the first list from head to tail, and `b` lists the second list from head to tail.\n\nThe lists share a suffix. Let `shared` be the number of nodes the two lists actually hold in common, `shared = min(tail, a.length, b.length)`. The last `shared` nodes of the second list are the very same physical nodes as the last `shared` nodes of the first list, so those shared nodes carry the values `a[a.length - shared]` through `a[a.length - 1]`. Capping `tail` at the shorter list is what keeps the description defined for every `tail` the constraints allow, including a `tail` larger than one of the lists. When `shared` is `0` the lists are disjoint and every node of `b` is an independent node.\n\nReturn the value stored in the first node that belongs to both lists (the intersection node), or `-1` when the lists are disjoint.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= a.length <= 200`, `1 <= b.length <= 200`, `-100 <= a[i] <= 100`, `-100 <= b[i] <= 100`, `0 <= tail <= a.length` and `0 <= tail <= b.length`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "a = [-100], b = [-100], tail = 0",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "a = [13, 40, -75], b = [66, 38], tail = 0",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(a: number[], b: number[], tail: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Both lists end in the same nodes, so the only difference between them is how many nodes come before the shared part.",
    "A shared suffix cannot be longer than the shorter list, so work out `shared = min(tail, a.length, b.length)` before anything else and make every implementation start from that same number.",
    "Once both sides agree on `shared`, advance the pointer of the longer list by the length difference, then move both pointers one step at a time and stop at the first step where both are inside the shared suffix."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force asks, for every node of the second list and every node of the first list, whether they are the same node, and returns the value of the first matching pair. Identity, not the value, is what makes a node shared, so this is O(n*m) with no extra memory. Both implementations start from the same shared-suffix length, `shared = max(0, min(tail, a.length, b.length))`, because that is the number of nodes the two lists can actually hold in common: an oversized `tail` cannot create more shared nodes than the shorter list owns, so both sides cap it to the shorter length before comparing anything. The optimal then uses the shape of the shared suffix: the lists differ only in their prefixes, so advancing the pointer of the longer list by the length difference puts both pointers the same distance from the end. Walking them in lockstep from there makes them reach the shared suffix on the same step, and the first step where both are inside the suffix is the intersection node. That costs O(n + m) time and two indices.",
    "why_optimal": "Both lists have to be walked at least up to the start of the shared suffix, so \u03a9(n + m) is a lower bound, and a real linked list offers nothing but next pointers, so no index arithmetic can skip the walk. The length-aligned twin pointer hits exactly that bound using constant extra space, beating the hash-set variant that would store every node of the first list. Correctness of the pair rests on both sides deriving the same `shared`; once they do, the brute force's identity map and the optimal's lockstep pointers describe exactly the same nodes, so they cannot disagree on any input the constraints allow. With lengths \u2264 200 the nested scan is still fast, which is why both implementations are acceptable on this judge.",
    "pitfalls": "Deriving the shared suffix from the raw `tail` on the brute-force side while the optimal caps it, which is the classic way the two drift apart: an oversized `tail` makes the brute force compute `bSharedStart = b.length - tail` and return a node, while the optimal tests `j === b.length - tail` and can never satisfy it, so it returns -1; cap `tail` to `min(tail, a.length, b.length)` once, identically, on both sides. Also: advancing the pointer of the shorter list instead of the longer one, so the pointers stay misaligned and the shared start is never detected; returning `b[m - shared]` rather than the owning node's value `a[n - shared]`; dropping the `shared === 0` guard, after which the loop can only fail to find the shared start and returns -1 by accident rather than by decision; treating a disjoint pair as an intersection and returning the first element instead of -1."
  },
